 ## Overview
-3D Gaussian Splatting Training is the iterative optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the photometric difference between rendered images and ground truth views [S2][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-fidelity radiance fields from sparse or dense image collections by replacing implicit neural networks with explicit, differentiable geometric primitives [S5, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF-based methods which require iterative volume rendering per pixel, 3DGS training leverages a differentiable rasterizer that projects Gaussians to 2D screen space, enabling significantly faster convergence and real-time rendering capabilities [S2][S5, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting Training is the iterative optimization process that adjusts the parameters of a set of 3D Gaussian primitives to minimize the photometric difference between rendered images and ground truth views [S2][S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of reconstructing high-fidelity 3D scenes from 2D images by replacing implicit neural fields with explicit, differentiable Gaussian splats [S5, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from NeRF-based methods by offering significantly faster training and inference speeds while achieving comparable image quality [S2][S5, unofficial]. Unlike static initialization methods, it employs adaptive density control to dynamically modify the primitive set during optimization [S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
